  "output": "I've now reviewed the full application (it's a small, deliberately server-rendered Next.js/Drizzle/Postgres app — not the sprawling codebase the checklist usually implies). Here's the honest picture: this code is disciplined, and most classic anti-patterns are already avoided by design. I found real issues in three areas and confirmed the other two categories are clean.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Category | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| N+1 queries | None found — all multi-table reads use joins or `Promise.all`. One related issue below (§2). |\n| Unnecessary re-renders | Minimal client-side state overall. One real O(n²) inefficiency (§3). |\n| Caching | One clear win: SQL aggregation instead of fetch-all-then-filter (§2). |\n| Memory leaks | None found — intervals and QueryClient are scoped/cleaned up correctly. |\n| Redundant computation | Same as §2 and §3. |\n\n---\n\n### 1. Missing indexes on foreign-key columns (biggest real issue)\n\n`lib/db/schema.ts` defines only one index (`entities_run_source_external_idx`). Postgres does **not** auto-index foreign keys — only the referenced primary-key side gets one for free. Every query that filters by these FKs will sequential-scan and get linearly worse as data grows:\n\n- `getSeverityBreakdown` — `conflicts.runId`\n- `listRunAudit` / `listRecentAudit` — `auditLog.runId`\n- `loadDecisions` (export) — joins through `matches.matchId`, `entities.id` via `entityAId`/`entityBId`\n- `loadSideAAccounts` — `entities.runId` + `entities.sourceId`\n- `resolutions.conflictId`\n\nThis is the schema-level equivalent of an N+1: not extra round-trips, but every one of these turning into a full-table scan once `entities`/`conflicts`/`audit_log` have real volume (they're per-run, unbounded tables).\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts\nexport const runs = pgTable(\"runs\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"runs_source_a_idx\").on(table.sourceAId),\n  index(\"runs_source_b_idx\").on(table.sourceBId),\n]);\n\nexport const entities = pgTable(\"entities\", { /* ...existing columns... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"entities_run_source_external_idx\").on(table.runId, table.sourceId, table.externalId),\n  index(\"entities_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId), // for loadSideAAccounts, getSeverityBreakdown-style scans\n]);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"conflicts_match_id_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"resolutions_conflict_id_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n```\nThen `npm run db:generate && npm run db:migrate`.\n\n### 2. `countSourcesBySide` fetches every row and Zod-parses it, then filters in JS three times\n\n`lib/server/sources.ts:24-31` calls `listSources()` (full select + `sourceSchema.parse` per row) just to produce three counts. The codebase already has the right pattern for this — `getSeverityBreakdown` in `lib/server/runs.ts` does the grouping in SQL. Apply the same pattern here:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { count, inArray } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, total: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n\n  const byKind = Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.kind, r.total]));\n  return {\n    crm: (byKind.salesforce ?? 0) + (byKind.hubspot ?? 0),\n    warehouse: (byKind.databricks ?? 0) + (byKind.snowflake ?? 0),\n    synthetic: byKind.synthetic ?? 0,\n  };\n}\n```\nLow-stakes today (sources is a small admin table), but it's the wrong pattern to have sitting next to the correct one, and it's what an engine grows into a real cost once someone adds a \"sources per integration\" dashboard on top of it.\n\n### 3. `RulesetEditor` does two `.indexOf()` scans per row → O(n²) render\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`:\n```ts\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);          // O(n)\n  return (\n    <RuleRow position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... />  // O(n) again\n  );\n})}\n```\nFor every row rendered, two linear scans over the full rule list. Harmless at today's rule counts, but it's needless — `orderRulesByEvaluation` already computes `{ rule, index }` pairs internally and throws them away. Carry the original index through instead:\n\n```ts\n// types/rules.ts\nexport function orderRulesByEvaluation(\n  rules: SurvivorshipRule[],\n): { rule: SurvivorshipRule; originalIndex: number }[] {\n  return rules\n    .map((rule, index) => ({ rule, index }))\n    .sort((a, b) => ruleSpecificity(b.rule) - ruleSpecificity(a.rule) || a.index - b.index)\n    .map(({ rule, index }, position) => ({ rule, originalIndex: index, position }));\n}\n```\n```tsx\n// ruleset-editor.tsx\n{ordered.map(({ rule, originalIndex, position }) => (\n  <RuleRow\n    key={rule.id}\n    rule={rule}\n    position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(originalIndex, patch)}\n    onRemove={rules.length > 1 ? () => removeRule(originalIndex) : undefined}\n  />\n))}\n```\n(Note: this changes `orderRulesByEvaluation`'s return shape — check for other call sites before applying; I didn't find any outside this component, but confirm before merging.)\n\n---\n\n### What I deliberately did *not* flag\n\n- **`sources`/`rulesets` reads use `connection()` on every request**, opting out of any caching. This looks like a target for `unstable_cache`, but the code comments explicitly justify it (source list is request-time state; rulesets must reflect the exact version a run reproduces against). Caching here would fight a documented correctness requirement, not just a perf one — I'd leave it unless these pages show up hot in real traffic.\n- **`RunLive`'s 2s poll** re-renders `RunProgress` every tick — that's the intended behavior of a live status view, not a bug, and it already stops polling and hands off to the server on terminal status (`components/runs/run-live.tsx:66-68`).\n- No unclosed intervals, subscriptions, or listeners anywhere — the one `setInterval` (`Elapsed` component) and the one `QueryClient` (`QueryProvider`) are both correctly scoped and cleaned up.\n- No N+1 query loops — every multi-row read I found uses a join or `Promise.all`, never a per-row query inside a loop.\n\nItem 1 (indexes) is the one worth prioritizing — it's the only finding with real growth-order consequences; 2 and 3 are small, low-risk cleanups.\n",
